[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1996","stub":{"id":"1996","section":"1. Shenandoah National  Park to Route 29","altname":"Upper Rapidan","river":"Rapidan","difficulty":"IItoIV","states":["USA-VRG"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"0.8","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"levelFT","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"110682","location":{"latitude":"38.4368","longitude":"-78.3662"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"115952","location":{"latitude":"38.2797","longitude":"-78.3415"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"15","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[],"detail":{"id":"1996","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"The Upper Rapidan and its nearby sibling the [Conway](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/1921/) are Class III whitewater runs that start at the Shenandoah National Park boundary. Both rivers suffered extensive damage in the 1995 flood and were essentially 'rebuilt', which means they were cleared of debris and a new cobbled river bed was created for them. Due to the gradient in the upper sections of these rivers, this makes for a pretty exciting first section.\n\nThe river overall has a variety of hazards, including fences, cattle gates and low water bridges.\n\nTo run the best 8 miles, use the alternate takeout at Rt. 230. Below this, the river calms down to a Class II. There is a dangerous low head dam right above Rt. 29 that should be portaged left.\n\nSee Also:\n\n_Classic Virginia Rivers,_ Ed Grove (1992 ed), p. 62.\n\n_Virginia Whitewater_, Roger Corbett (2000 ed.), p. 166.\n\n_Exploring Virginia's Waterways_, Ed Gertler (2022 ed.), p. 125.","averageGradient":"20","maxGradient":"85","length":"15.7","correlations":[{"reachID":"1996","isPrimary":true,"co